# libcss: math functions nest, and a divisor may be followed by whitespace (patch 326)
# Generated 2026-10-29
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 325)
#
# wordpress.org/news (scorecard 74.8, the weakest scored site in r59) sets
# its spacing preset to `clamp( 24px, calc( 100vw / 18 ), 80px )`. Every
# padding and margin built on it parsed as invalid, so the page's columns
# lost their gutters and the two-column split moved.
#
#  - parse/properties/utils.c css__parse_calc_value(): a calc(), min(),
#    max() or clamp() inside an expression is a FUNCTION token, not a '('.
#    It was rejected, and with it the whole declaration. Its arguments now
#    parse into the same bytecode buffer through
#    css__parse_calc_func_args(), split out of css__parse_calc(). The
#    evaluator (select/calc.c) is a stack machine, so the nested function's
#    terminal operator leaves one value for the outer expression.
#  - css__parse_calc_product(): whitespace after a divisor is consumed,
#    as css__parse_calc_value() does for every other operand.
#    `calc( 100vw / 18 )` failed on the space before its ')'.
#
# Gate: CSS-calc-nested (CALCNEST326), legs 1-7 for this patch (the
# clamp/calc nesting, the spaces, the var() chain, min(max(calc())),
# calc(10px + min())). All fail without it.
